1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,200
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,900
I built a system that takes a video idea,

3
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,950
plans and generates the shots,

4
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:07,000
reviews the results,

5
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,600
and turns everything into a finished video.

6
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:11,850
For this demo, I'll quickly show you

7
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:13,100
the process and the final output.

8
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:16,150
The process starts with a simple request.

9
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:17,950
Instead of giving Conductor

10
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:19,200
a long prompt every time,

11
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,000
I moved the recurring instructions

12
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,100
into the workflow.

13
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:24,400
Now I can give it a short request,

14
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,850
and the system handles the rest of the process.

15
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:30,600
The first thing the system does

16
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:32,300
is look at this existing video library.

17
00:00:32,450 --> 00:00:34,600
This library gives the system examples

18
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:36,350
of different video styles,

19
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:38,750
pacing, transitions, and storytelling.

20
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:41,400
It uses those references as inspiration

21
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:42,600
to plan a new video,

22
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,450
rather than copying one directly.

23
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,000
After reviewing the references,

24
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,200
the system breaks the idea into individual shots.

25
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:52,450
For this video, it created four shots

26
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:54,400
with their own purpose, timing,

27
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,400
copy, and visual direction.

28
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:58,850
Here, I am showing three of them:

29
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:00,000
the initial brief,

30
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,150
the campaign idea,

31
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:02,800
and how that idea expands

32
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,050
into different marketing assets.

33
00:01:05,550 --> 00:01:08,350
For this project, the system chose HyperFrames

34
00:01:08,350 --> 00:01:09,800
because the video needed

35
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:12,000
precise text, UI, and layout

36
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,300
instead of generative footage.

37
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:16,900
For other videos, it can use models

38
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:18,150
available through Fal

39
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:20,050
when the shot needs things like

40
00:01:20,050 --> 00:01:22,600
cinematic movement or generated footage.

41
00:01:23,050 --> 00:01:24,850
The idea is to choose the method

42
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:26,100
that best fits the shot,

43
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:27,800
instead of using the same model

44
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:28,800
for everything.

45
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:31,300
Once the individual shots are ready,

46
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:34,000
they're combined into the final video.

47
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:36,400
The workflow then checks things like

48
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:38,000
timing, transitions, audio,

49
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:39,700
and the final output

50
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:42,000
before considering the video complete.

51
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:44,100
For every finished video,

52
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:45,400
the system creates its own

53
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:47,100
reverse-engineering document.

54
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:50,050
This records how the video was made,

55
00:01:50,050 --> 00:01:52,650
including the prompts, structure,

56
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:54,100
methods used, revisions,

57
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:55,450
and key learnings.

58
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:57,300
The workflow also checks

59
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:58,550
that this file exists

60
00:01:58,550 --> 00:02:01,050
before the video can be marked complete.

61
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:03,250
And this is the final video

62
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:05,250
produced through that workflow.

63
00:02:42,450 --> 00:02:44,550
The next phase I'm working on

64
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:45,750
is making people look

65
00:02:45,750 --> 00:02:47,150
and move more naturally,

66
00:02:47,150 --> 00:02:48,600
especially their hands,

67
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:50,400
eye movement, and longer scenes.

68
00:02:51,050 --> 00:02:53,150
I'm keeping that research separate for now

69
00:02:53,150 --> 00:02:55,500
while I continue testing and improving it.
